\id PHP Bench: the layouts a typist meets
\usfm 3.1
\h Edges
\mt1 Edges
\c 1
\s1 A verse that runs across paragraphs
\p
\v 1 This verse starts in one paragraph
\p and ends in the next.
\v 2 A second verse shares that paragraph.
\s1 Poetry across verses
\q1
\v 3 The first line of a verse,
\q2 its second line,
\q1 and a third.
\q1
\v 4 One line of its own.
\b
\p
\v 5 After a blank line.
\s1 Empty verses
\p
\v 6
\v 7
\v 8 Text after two empty verses.
\s1 Ranges, notes and odd numbers
\p
\v 9-10 A bridged verse.
\v 11 A verse ending in a footnote.\f + \fr 1:11: \ft The note's text.\f*
\v 12 A cross reference\x - \xo 1:12: \xt Rom 1:1\x* mid-verse.
\v 14 Thirteen is missing.
\v 13 And comes late.
\v 14 Fourteen again.
\c 2
\cl Chapter Two
\p
\v 1 A short chapter, one paragraph,
\v 2 two verses.
\s1 A table
\p
\v 3 The census of the tribes:
\tr \th1 Tribe \thr2 Number
\tr \tc1 Reuben \tcr2 46,500
\tr \tc1 Simeon \tcr2 59,300
\tr \tc1 Gad \tcr2 45,650
\tr \tc1 Judah, the largest \tcr2 74,600
\p
\v 4 And the counting ended.
\c 3
\s1 A heading before anything else
\m
\v 1 Continues in a margin paragraph.
\p
\v 2 Ends the book without a newline.